 originated in Jamaica, specifically in Kingston, during the late 1960s."}], "places": [{"name": "Kingston, Jamaica", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Montego Bay, Jamaica", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "London, United Kingdom", "role": "Major Influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: reggae", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "reggae"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q9794", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reggae"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "reggae left a notable mark on Contemporary history, documented across 121 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Reggae has significantly shaped global music and cultural identity, influencing various genres and spawning movements for social justice, particularly within the African diaspora.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-07T13:06:17Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27093366438", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Music genre that originated in jamaica in the late 1960s Created c. 1961.", "newValue": "Reggae is a music genre that emerged in Jamaica during the late 1960s, specifically around 1968, characterized by its distinct rhythmic style and socially conscious lyrics. The genre has roots in earlier Jamaican musical forms such as ska and rocksteady, and it played a critical role in the cultural expression of the Rastafarian movement. Key figures in the development of reggae include Bob Marley, Peter Tosh, and Jimmy Cliff, whose contributions helped to popularize the genre beyond Jamaica, making it a global phenomenon.

The rise of reggae was marked by significant events such as the release of Bob Marley's iconic album "Catch a Fire" in 1973, which introduced reggae to an international audience. Reggae music often addresses themes of social justice, resistance against oppression, and the celebration of African heritage. The genre also laid the groundwork for the emergence of dancehall music in the late 1970s and 1980s, which further evolved the Jamaican music scene. Festivals and concerts, such as the Reggae Sunsplash, have been instrumental in showcasing reggae talent and promoting its cultural significance worldwide.

